📊 Function-by-Function Summary
Function 1 (1D / sparse peaks)
Nature: near-zero everywhere, sharp local peaks
Key issue: noise + very local structure
Model choice:
small length_scale
careful alpha (avoid overfitting noise)
Acquisition: UCB or EI

Insight:

GP must capture sharp local behaviour; uncertainty plays a big role early on.

Function 2 (2D / noisy, multimodal)
Nature: noisy + multiple local optima
Model choice:
higher alpha (e.g. 1e-2)
flexible length scale
Acquisition: EI preferred

Insight:

Exploration is important to avoid getting stuck in local optima.

Function 3 (3D / transformed objective)
Nature: maximise transformed (negative) objective
Key point:
maximise as given (no re-negation)
Challenge: visualisation
Solution:
2D slices (fix one dimension)

Insight:

BO works identically even when all values are negative.

Function 4 (4D / complex, many local optima)
Nature: highly multimodal, expensive evaluations
Model choice:
moderate/high alpha
Acquisition: EI or exploratory UCB
Search: random candidates

Insight:

Strong exploration needed due to many competing optima.

Function 5 (4D / unimodal)
Nature: mostly single peak
Model choice:
smoother kernel (larger length scale)
lower exploration
Acquisition:
EI with small xi
or UCB with small beta

Insight:

Exploitation is more appropriate than broad exploration.

Function 6 (5D / negative scoring system)
Nature: all outputs negative by design
Key point:
maximise directly (closest to zero)
Model choice:
moderate noise
Search: random candidates

Insight:

BO only cares about ordering, not absolute sign.

Function 7 (6D / ML hyperparameters)
Nature: moderate dimensional hyperparameter tuning
Model choice:
anisotropic RBF
learned length scales per dimension
Observation:
some dimensions very smooth (large length scale)
others sensitive
Acquisition: UCB

Insight:

GP learns which hyperparameters matter most.

Function 8 (8D / high-dimensional tuning)
Nature: high-dimensional, complex, local maxima sufficient
Model choice:
anisotropic RBF
Search:
large random candidate set
Acquisition: EI
Observation:
some dimensions effectively flat

Insight:

In high dimensions, BO becomes approximate; finding strong local optima is sufficient.

🔑 Cross-Cutting Insights
1. Length scale interpretation
small → sensitive dimension
large → smooth / less important dimension
hitting upper bound → dimension varies weakly
2. Noise (alpha)
too small → overfitting
too large → oversmoothing
noisy problems need larger alpha
3. UCB vs EI
Method	Behaviour
UCB	simple, tunable exploration
EI	focuses on improving best value
4. Dimensionality effect
Dim	Strategy
1–2	dense grid OK
3	grid or slices
4–6	random candidates
7–8	large random sampling, approximate